('B', '**Bulimia and binge-eating disorder:** therapist-led CBT beat wait-lists and other psychotherapies, was most effective in its manualized and enhanced forms, matched antidepressants at the end of treatment, and outperformed interpersonal therapy on symptoms after treatment (Linardon et al., 2017). CBT has kept its status as first-line therapy for bulimia (Hay, 2013).'),
('B', '**Transdiagnostic CBT** (CBT-E) treats the shared core of all eating disorders (Fairburn et al., 2003). In a trial with 154 outpatients who were not markedly underweight, it produced large, lasting improvement, and 51.3% were near the community norm at 60 weeks, regardless of diagnosis (Fairburn et al., 2009).'),
('B', '**Binge-eating disorder:** psychotherapy, mostly CBT, had large effects on binge eating, and CBT-based self-help had medium-to-large effects; medications had small effects (Hilbert et al., 2019). Lisdexamfetamine at 50 to 70 mg reduced binge days compared with placebo (McElroy et al., 2015).'),
('B', '**Adolescent anorexia:** family-based treatment produced more full remission than individual therapy at 6 and 12 months (Lock et al., 2010).'),
